# Allow-list: `dof adopt` imports only these paths from the home directory.
#
# The target is ~ itself because zsh insists on finding its startup files
# there — there is no XDG path it will read without ZDOTDIR being exported
# from somewhere even earlier. So the allow-list is doing real work here:
# without it, `dof adopt zsh` would walk all of $HOME.
#
# Always scope the adopt anyway — `dof adopt zsh .zshrc`, never a bare
# `dof adopt zsh`. The allow-list filters the *result* of the find; it does
# not stop find from traversing every file under the home directory first.
#
# ── Deliberately NOT listed ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# .zprofile      Also on this machine, also carrying a hardcoded PATH entry.
#                Left alone until it is looked at on its own terms; adopting
#                it as a side effect of the .zshrc work is how state files
#                end up in git (see notes/ai-context.md).
# .zsh_history   Command history. Machine-local, and reads like a diary.
# .zshenv .zlogin .zlogout
#                Do not exist here. Listing files that do not exist is how
#                the agy allow-list grew a phantom GEMINI.md entry.
# .oh-my-zsh/    Cloned by its own installer, ~2000 files, updates itself.

.zshrc
